  <!-- Services Detail -->
  <section id="services" class="bg-[#F8F8F5] py-20">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6">
      <div class="text-center mb-14">
        <p class="text-[#9A9D62] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-sm mb-3">What We Handle</p>
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-4">
          Ignition Repair Services in Delaware
        </h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 max-w-3xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          Ignition problems present in different ways and for different reasons. Here is a detailed look at every ignition repair scenario we handle and how we approach each one professionally on-site throughout Delaware.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-8">

        <!-- Stiff or Sticky Ignition -->
        <div id="stiff-ignition" class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-gray-200 hover:shadow-lg transition">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-[#A61E22]/10 rounded-2xl flex items-center justify-center mb-6">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-7 h-7 text-[#A61E22]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M10.325 4.317c.426-1.756 2.924-1.756 3.35 0a1.724 1.724 0 002.573 1.066c1.543-.94 3.31.826 2.37 2.37a1.724 1.724 0 001.065 2.572c1.756.426 1.756 2.924 0 3.35a1.724 1.724 0 00-1.066 2.573c.94 1.543-.826 3.31-2.37 2.37a1.724 1.724 0 00-2.572 1.065c-.426 1.756-2.924 1.756-3.35 0a1.724 1.724 0 00-2.573-1.066c-1.543.94-3.31-.826-2.37-2.37a1.724 1.724 0 00-1.065-2.572c-1.756-.426-1.756-2.924 0-3.35a1.724 1.724 0 001.066-2.573c-.94-1.543.826-3.31 2.37-2.37.996.608 2.296.07 2.572-1.065z" />
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M15 12a3 3 0 11-6 0 3 3 0 016 0z" />
            </svg>
          </div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">Stiff, Sticky, or Gritty Ignition Cylinder Repair in Delaware</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              One of the most common ignition complaints we hear is a key that has started to feel stiff, resistant, or slightly gritty when turning. This is typically an early sign of internal wear inside the ignition cylinder — the tumblers and wafers are losing their smooth action, grime and debris have built up inside the cylinder housing, or the lubricant has dried out after years of use. The good news is that in many cases this is entirely repairable without replacing the whole cylinder.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              Our technicians will carefully inspect the ignition cylinder, clean out any debris or dried-up lubricant from inside the housing, apply professional-grade locksmith lubricant to restore smooth operation, and test thoroughly before leaving. If the internal wear is too advanced for lubrication alone, we will assess whether tumbler adjustment or cylinder replacement is the right next step — and we will tell you honestly which one applies.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              Address a stiff ignition early — before it progresses to a key that won't turn at all. Call 1st Capital Locksmith for on-site ignition repair throughout Delaware.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Key Won't Turn -->
        <div id="key-wont-turn" class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-gray-200 hover:shadow-lg transition">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-[#A61E22]/10 rounded-2xl flex items-center justify-center mb-6">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-7 h-7 text-[#A61E22]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M18.364 18.364A9 9 0 005.636 5.636m12.728 12.728A9 9 0 015.636 5.636m12.728 12.728L5.636 5.636" />
            </svg>
          </div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">Key Won't Turn in the Ignition — Diagnosis & Repair</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              A key that inserts into the ignition but absolutely will not turn is a stressful and disruptive problem. Before assuming the worst, there are a few things to check. First, try gently rocking the steering wheel left and right while simultaneously attempting to turn the key — a locked steering column is a very common cause of this and is easily released by relieving the pressure on the ignition column. If that works, there may be nothing wrong with the ignition itself.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              If the steering column is not the issue, the most likely causes are a worn ignition cylinder where the tumblers are no longer aligning with the key's cuts, or a worn key that is no longer the correct profile to operate the cylinder. Our technicians will assess both the cylinder and the key, and in many cases we can repair or recondition the cylinder so it works correctly again — or cut a fresh key if the original has worn down.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              Do not force the key or use excessive pressure — this can snap the key in the ignition and create a much bigger and more expensive problem. Call 1st Capital Locksmith and we will come to you anywhere in Delaware.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Key Won't Come Out -->
        <div id="key-wont-come-out" class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-gray-200 hover:shadow-lg transition">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-[#A61E22]/10 rounded-2xl flex items-center justify-center mb-6">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-7 h-7 text-[#A61E22]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M12 15v2m-6 4h12a2 2 0 002-2v-6a2 2 0 00-2-2H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v6a2 2 0 002 2zm10-10V7a4 4 0 00-8 0v4h8z" />
            </svg>
          </div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">Key Won't Come Out of the Ignition in Delaware</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              A key that goes in, starts the car normally, but then refuses to come back out of the ignition is a surprisingly common problem — and one that people tend to work around for far too long before calling for help. In automatic transmission vehicles, a shift interlock mechanism is designed to prevent the key from being removed unless the transmission is fully in park. If the interlock is not releasing properly, the key will stay stuck even with the transmission in park.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              If the shift interlock is functioning correctly, the cause is usually inside the ignition cylinder itself — a worn or failed release mechanism that is no longer allowing the key to disengage and come free. Our technicians will identify the root cause: if it is an ignition cylinder repair issue, we will repair or replace the affected component. If it is a shift interlock problem, we can diagnose that as well and advise on the appropriate repair.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              Do not try to force the key out with pliers or other tools — you risk snapping the key and causing considerably more damage. Call us for safe, professional ignition repair throughout Delaware.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Broken Key in Ignition -->
        <div id="broken-key-ignition" class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-gray-200 hover:shadow-lg transition">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-[#A61E22]/10 rounded-2xl flex items-center justify-center mb-6">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-7 h-7 text-[#A61E22]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M15 7a2 2 0 012 2m4 0a6 6 0 01-7.743 5.743L11 17H9v2H7v2H4a1 1 0 01-1-1v-2.586a1 1 0 01.293-.707l5.964-5.964A6 6 0 1121 9z" />
            </svg>
          </div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">Broken Key Extraction from the Ignition in Delaware</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              When a worn or weakened key breaks off inside the ignition, it creates an immediate problem — the vehicle cannot be started, and the broken piece blocks any other key from entering. This situation almost always happens without warning, often during a routine start when the key snaps under the torsional force of turning. It is more common in keys that have developed small stress cracks over years of use, or when someone applies excessive force to a stiff ignition.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              Our locksmiths have specialized extraction tools designed specifically for removing broken key fragments from ignition cylinders. We work carefully to remove the broken piece without damaging the ignition cylinder itself — which preserves the cylinder and avoids the need for full replacement in most cases. Once the fragment is removed, we assess the cylinder's condition and <a href="/services/car-key-replacement/" class="text-[#A61E22] hover:underline font-medium">cut a new key</a> to replace the broken one.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              Never attempt to remove a broken key fragment using tweezers, glue, or improvised tools — these approaches usually push the fragment deeper or cause additional damage. Call 1st Capital Locksmith for professional <a href="/services/broken-key-extraction/" class="text-[#A61E22] hover:underline font-medium">broken key extraction</a> throughout Delaware.
            </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Transponder & Ignition Communication Issues -->
        <div id="transponder-ignition" class="bg-white rounded-2xl p-8 border border-gray-200 hover:shadow-lg transition">
          <div class="w-14 h-14 bg-[#A61E22]/10 rounded-2xl flex items-center justify-center mb-6">
            <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-7 h-7 text-[#A61E22]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
              <path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M8.111 16.404a5.5 5.5 0 017.778 0M12 20h.01m-7.08-7.071c3.904-3.905 10.236-3.905 14.141 0M1.394 9.393c5.857-5.857 15.355-5.857 21.213 0" />
            </svg>
          </div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mb-3">Transponder Key & Ignition Communication Issues in Delaware</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              Most vehicles manufactured after the mid-1990s use transponder keys — keys with a small electronic chip embedded in the plastic head that communicates with the vehicle's immobilizer system when you turn the ignition. If this communication fails, the engine may crank but will not start, or the security light on the dashboard may stay on. This is a different kind of ignition problem to a mechanical cylinder issue, but it presents very similarly from the driver's perspective.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-3">
              Common causes include a damaged or de-programmed transponder chip, a failing ignition reader antenna (the ring around the ignition barrel that reads the chip), or a key that has been copied onto a non-transponder blank. Our technicians can test the transponder chip, the ignition reader, and the key itself to identify which component has failed and what the correct repair is.
            </p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              If your car cranks but won't start and the security light is illuminated, a transponder communication issue is a likely cause. Call 1st Capital Locksmith and we will diagnose it on-site throughout Delaware.
            </p>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Photo Gallery Strip -->
  <section class="bg-white py-16">
    <div class="max-w-6xl mx-auto px-6">
      <div class="text-center mb-10">
        <p class="text-[#9A9D62] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-sm mb-3">On-Site Ignition Repair</p>
        <h2 class="text-2xl md:text-3xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22]">Diagnosed & Repaired at Your Location — Anywhere in Delaware</h2>
      </div>
      <!-- PHOTO SLOT 4, 5, 6: Recommended 600×400px each
