  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="bg-white py-20">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
      <div class="text-center mb-12">
        <p class="text-[#9A9D62] font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-sm mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</p>
        <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22]">
          Ignition Repair Questions — Answered
        </h2>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
          <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
            How do I know if my ignition needs repair or full replacement?
            <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            In general, if the ignition cylinder is stiff, sticky, or slightly worn but physically intact, repair is often possible — cleaning, lubrication, and minor tumbler adjustment can restore smooth operation. If the cylinder is severely worn, cracked, broken, or has been tampered with or damaged in a theft attempt, replacement is usually the better long-term solution. Our technicians will assess the cylinder on-site and give you an honest recommendation. We only push for replacement when repair genuinely is not the right call.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
          <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
            Can I use WD-40 to fix a stiff ignition?
            <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            WD-40 is not a recommended lubricant for ignition cylinders. It is a water displacer and light solvent, not a proper lock lubricant. While it may temporarily loosen a stiff cylinder, it tends to attract and trap debris over time and can actually make the problem worse with repeated use. For ignition cylinders, professional locksmiths use graphite-based or PTFE dry lubricants that are specifically designed for precision lock mechanisms. If your ignition is stiff, call us — we use the correct products and can solve the problem properly.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
          <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
            My key won't turn in the ignition — what should I try first?
            <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            Before calling us, try this: with your foot off the brake, gently rock the steering wheel left and right while simultaneously turning the key. A locked steering column is one of the most common causes of a key that won't turn, and this simple technique often releases it immediately. If that doesn't work, check that the transmission is fully in park (for automatics). If neither solves the problem, stop attempting to force the key — excessive force can break the key in the cylinder — and call 1st Capital Locksmith to diagnose the issue properly.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
          <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
            My car cranks but won't start and the security light is on — is that an ignition problem?
            <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            Yes, this is often a transponder or immobilizer issue related to the ignition system. When the security light stays illuminated and the engine cranks but won't fire, it typically means the vehicle's immobilizer system is not recognizing the transponder chip in your key. This can happen due to a failed chip in the key, a damaged ignition reader antenna, or a programming issue. Call us and we can test the transponder and ignition reader on-site throughout Delaware and reprogram or replace the affected component.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
          <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
            How long does ignition repair take?
            <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            For straightforward ignition cylinder cleaning and lubrication, we are typically done within 20–30 minutes on-site. More involved repairs — tumbler adjustment, broken key extraction, or transponder issue diagnosis and resolution — may take 45 minutes to an hour depending on the vehicle and the specific problem. We work efficiently and will give you a realistic estimate when we arrive and assess the situation.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
          <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
            Can you repair the ignition on any make or model of vehicle?
            <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-4 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
            We work on a very wide range of domestic and foreign vehicles including Toyota, Honda, Ford, Chevrolet, Dodge, GMC, Jeep, RAM, Nissan, Hyundai, Kia, Subaru, Volkswagen, BMW, Mercedes-Benz, and many more. If you are unsure whether we can help with your specific vehicle, call us and we will let you know upfront before you book the visit.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-[#F8F8F5] rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-200 group">
          <summary class="font-bold text-[#1A1F22] text-lg cursor-pointer flex justify-between items-center">
            Do you come to me for ignition repair in Delaware?
            <span class="text-[#A61E22] text-2xl group-open:rotate-45 transition-transform">+</span>
